{"agent":"fix-review-triage-flags","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"The assertion `assert \"REQUIRES the\\n`triage` verb\" in p.stdout` hard-codes a newline at one specific position inside a help sentence. argparse's `HelpFormatter` computes its wrap width from `shutil.get_terminal_size().columns - 2`, and `shutil.get_terminal_size()` reads the `COLUMNS` environment variable before falling back to 80. The subprocess inherits the environment (`env={**os.environ, ...}`), so if `COLUMNS` is exported to anything other than the value that reproduces the exact wrap point the test author saw (e.g. `COLUMNS=120` in a CI runner or developer shell), the description is re-wrapped and `REQUIRES the ` + \"`triage` verb\" ends up on a single line, so the substring with the embedded `\\n` no longer matches and the test fails even though the CLI help is correct and the feature works. The same assertion also silently depends on all preceding text in that help paragraph having exactly the width it had when the expected string was pasted. A robust form would normalize whitespace (e.g. assert on `\" \".join(p.stdout.split())`) rather than on a raw newline.","digest":"988dd79eec8ba4b4","location":"tests/test_review_triage_flags.py::test_help_says_the_verb_is_required","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_review_triage_flags.py::test_help_says_the_verb_is_required"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","diff_chars":9137,"diff_sha":"490b20ff3f6269aaa334fbb18d2dbcaa8d1f4066ab3e14c30984066802ad5bc8","diff_source":"main..HEAD + working tree in /home/delian/src/.ddflow-worktrees/B-fix-review-triage-flags","elapsed_s":236.2,"family":"deepseek","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","reason":"","reviewed":[1,2],"reviewer":"lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewer_digest":"d34b8af1b4a590b5","status":"REVIEWED","titles":["tests/test_review_triage_flags.py::test_help_says_the_verb_i